15. Hypergeometric Functions
Mathematical Properties
15.1. Gauss Series, Special Elementary Cases, Special Values of the Argument
Gauss Series
The circle of convergence of the Gauss hypergeometric series
15.1.1  F(a, b; c; z)
=F(b, a; c; a)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(a)_{n}(b)_{n}}{(c)_{n}}\frac{z^{n}}{n!}\]
is the unit circle |z|=1. The behavior of this series on its circle of convergence is:
1. Divergence when \(\mathcal{R}\left(c-a-b\right)\leq-1\).
2. Absolute convergence when \(\mathcal{R}\left(e-a-b\right)>0\).
3. Conditional convergence when \(-1<\mathcal{R}\left(e-a-b\right)\leq0\); the point \(z=1\) is excluded. The Gauss series reduces to a polynomial of degree n in z when a or b is equal to -n, (n=0, 1, 2, \ldots). (For these cases see also 15.4.) The series 15.1.1 is not defined when c is equal to -m, (m=0, 1, 2, \ldots), provided a or b is not a negative integer n with n<m. For e=-m
15.1.2 \lim_{\epsilon\to-m}\frac{1}{\Gamma(c)}\, F(a, b; c; z)= \frac{(a)_{n+1}(b)_{n+1}}{(m+1)!}z^{n+1}F\left(a+m+1, b+m+1; m+2; z\right)
Special Elementary Cases of Gauss Series
(For cases involving higher functions see 15.4.)
15.1.3   F(1, 1; 2; z)=z^{-1}\ln\left(1-z\right)
15.1.4   F(1, 1; 3; z')=\tfrac{1}{2}z^{-1}\ln\left(\tfrac{1+z}{1-z}\right)
15.1.5   F(1, 1; 3; -z')=z^{-1}\arctan z
15.1.6   F(1, 1; 2; z')=(1-z')F(1, 1; 3; z')=z^{-1}\arcsin z
556
15.1.7   F(1, 1; 3; 4; -z')=(1+z')F(1, 1; 1; 2; -z')=z^{-1}\ln[z+(1+z')]
15.1.8   F(a, b; b; z)=(1-z)^{-a}
15.1.9   F(a, 1; 2+a; 3; z')=\tfrac{1}{2}[(1+z)^{-w}+(1-z)^{-w}]
15.1.10   F(a, 1; 2+a; 3; z')=\tfrac{1}{2}z^{-1}(1-2a)^{-1}[(1+z)^{1-w}-(1-z)^{1-w}]
15.1.11   F(-a, a; 3; -z')=\tfrac{1}{2}[(1+z')^{1+z'}+[(1+z')^{1-z'}]^{2w}]
15.1.12   F(a, 1-a; 3; -z')=\tfrac{1}{2}[(1+z')^{1+z'}]=\tfrac{1}{2}[(1+z')^{1-z'}]=\tfrac{1}{2}[(1+z')^{1-z'}]=2^{w-1}\)
15.1.13   F(a, 1; 2+a; 2)=2^{w}[1+(1-z)^{1}]=2^{w}[1-(1-z)^{1}]=\tfrac{1}{2}[(1-z)^{1}F(1+a, 1; 2+a; 1+2a; z)]
15.1.14   F(a, 1; 2+a; 2a; z)=2^{w-1}[1-z)^{-1}[1+(1-z')^{1-w}]
15.1.15   F(a, 1--a; 3; \sin^{2}z)=\tfrac{\sin[(2a-1)z]}{(2a-1)\sin z}
15.1.16   F(a, 2--a; 3; \sin^{2}z)=\tfrac{\sin[(2a-2)z]}{(a-1)\sin(2)}
15.1.17   F(-a, a; 1; \sin^{2}z)=cos(2a)
15.1.18   F(a, 1--a; 3; \sin^{2}z)=\tfrac{\cos[(2a-1)z]}{\cos z}
15.1.19   F(a, 1; 2+a; 1; --\tan^{2}z)=\cos^{2h}z\cos(2az)
